  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nMostly cloudy skies, a bit of drizzle and cool temperatures\nlingered across the local area today. An upper level low across\neastern Wyoming on Satellite is helping to trigger a few\nthunderstorms across the high plains - which if model data is\ncorrect, should mostly dissipate at they approach the area late\nthis evening as they move into a more stable environment. \n\nOvernight, a few storms could cross areas mainly north of I-80,\ncloser to the center of the low, as it tracks east across the \ncentral plains. If realized, the environment remains \nunfavorable for strong or severe storms due to a lack of \ninstability, and any storms should remain isolated and on the \nweaker side. Of secondary concern, there are some hints of fog \ndevelopment across the area to start the day Monday with the \nlatest HRRR indicating some widely scattered dense fog. Given \nthe light northeasterly winds and minimal T/Td spread, cannot \nrule out at least some patchy fog formation, and consequently \nadded fog to the forecast to start the day Monday.\n\nWhile some small pops remain in the forecast for Monday, most of\nthe local area should remain dry behind the departing upper\nlevel low, with these mostly dry conditions likely prevailing\nthrough at least midday Wednesday, as weak ridging sets up\nacross the local area ahead of the next more notable\ndisturbance. While some small splotchy pops remain in the\nforecast at times late Monday through Wednesday afternoon, model\nsignals for precip mid-week with this next disturbance have \nreally ramped up over the past 24 hours, with most of ensemble \nmembers indicating a potentially very wet Wed/Thursday time \nperiod. While surface temperatures remain on the cooler side of\nclimo and instability is on the lower side, could see some\nembedded thunderstorms Wednesday night through Thursday, with\nmore showery and less widespread precip expected to end the week\nand continue into next weekend. \n\nOverall, a seasonably cool and occasionally unsettled period is\nforecast to continue across the local area for much of the next\n7-10 days, with lots of cloud cover and the potential for some\nmorning fog at times.",
